{
  "id": "vlach_roma_djili",
  "name": "Vlach Roma song (loki and khelimaski djili)",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Vocal music of the Vlach (Oláh) Roma of Hungary and neighbouring countries, Romani-speaking Lovari, Kalderash and related groups who, unlike the Romungro restaurant musicians, made music largely without instruments. Slow songs (loki djili) are sung solo or by a group in free rhythm, while dance songs (khelimaski djili) are carried by voices, an oral bass of rhythmic vocables (szájbőgő), finger snaps and the beating of a water can and spoons; the vocal percussion card stands for the oral bass and the credited auxiliary percussion card for the can. Rudolf Víg and Katalin Kovalcsik documented the repertoire, and Kalyi Jag and Ando Drom brought it to stage and studio from the late 1970s.",
  "recipe": "classic 1977-1979 vlach roma song, ballad Poetry. single leader group response. vocal percussion ensemble layer humming melody simultaneous percussion dissociated airflow vocal percussion voice, credited auxiliary percussion, spoons metal spoons. field recording. small-diaphragm pencil condenser, portable field-recorder pre. tape 15ips.",
